WB.L("illusory-truth-effect", "thinking", "beginner", "The illusory truth effect: why repetition feels like proof", "Hear something often enough and it starts to feel true, even when you know better. Here's why your brain falls for it, and how to fight back.",
  [
    ["Three sessions in 1977", "Simply repeating a statement makes people rate it as more likely to be true.",
      "In 1977 the psychologists Lynn Hasher, David Goldstein and Thomas Toppino ran a simple experiment with university students. Over three sessions, two weeks apart, the students read a long list of trivia statements and rated how confident they were that each one was true. Some statements were true and some were false, and they were obscure enough that most people couldn't know for sure. The twist was that some statements appeared in every session, while others were brand new each time. The repeated statements were rated as more and more likely to be true with each session, whether they were actually true or not. Nothing about the evidence had changed. Only familiarity had. The researchers had documented what is now called the illusory truth effect."],
    ["Fluency feels like truth", "Familiar ideas are easier for the brain to process, and that ease gets mistaken for accuracy.",
      "Why should repetition matter? The leading explanation involves something psychologists call processing fluency. A statement you have met before is quicker and easier to read and understand, because your brain has already done some of the work. That smooth, effortless feeling is normally a decent clue: things we hear often are frequently true, because true things tend to be repeated by many independent sources. So the mind takes a shortcut and treats ease as a sign of accuracy. The trouble is that fluency can come from almost anywhere. Studies have found that statements printed in easier-to-read colours are judged more likely to be true, and that rhyming sayings, such as ‘woes unite foes’, are rated as more accurate than the same ideas phrased without a rhyme."],
    ["Knowing better doesn't protect you", "Repetition can nudge belief even in claims that contradict what you already know.",
      "It's tempting to think this only works on topics you know nothing about. A 2015 study led by the psychologist Lisa Fazio tested that idea. Participants read statements such as ‘A sari is the name of the short pleated skirt worn by Scots’, which contradicts something most of them knew: that skirt is a kilt. Even so, reading a false statement more than once made people rate it as somewhat more true than they otherwise would. Knowledge helped, but it didn't switch the effect off. People often failed to bring what they knew to mind, relying on the feeling of familiarity instead. Later research has found that the biggest jump in belief tends to come from the second exposure, with smaller gains after that."],
    ["Fake news and the repeat button", "Even a single earlier glimpse of a false headline can make it seem more accurate later.",
      "In 2018 Gordon Pennycook, Tyrone Cannon and David Rand showed people real fake-news headlines from social media, styled as they would appear in a feed. Later, the headlines people had seen before were rated as more accurate than ones they were seeing for the first time, after just one earlier exposure. Strikingly, the effect still appeared when the headlines had carried a warning label saying they were disputed by fact-checkers. This matters because online platforms are repetition machines. A claim can reach you through a friend's post, a meme, a video and a comment thread in the same afternoon, and each one makes it feel a little more familiar. Advertisers and propagandists have understood the power of repetition for a very long time."],
    ["The trouble with myth-busting", "Repeating a myth to debunk it can accidentally spread it, so lead with the truth instead.",
      "This creates a headache for anyone trying to correct a falsehood. A typical myth-busting article repeats the myth, sometimes in large type, then explains why it's wrong. Readers may later remember the familiar claim and forget the correction. Researchers once worried that corrections might routinely ‘backfire’ and strengthen false beliefs, but later work found this is rare; corrections generally do help. Still, how you correct matters. A widely recommended approach, sometimes called a ‘truth sandwich’, starts with the accurate fact, mentions the myth once and clearly labels it as false, explains why it's wrong, then finishes by repeating the truth. That way the fact, not the myth, is the thing that gets the repetition and becomes familiar."],
    ["Defending your own mind", "Pausing to ask how you actually know something helps separate familiarity from evidence.",
      "You can't stop repetition from affecting you, but you can make it work less well. One useful habit is to notice the feeling of ‘I've heard that before’ and treat it as a cue, not a conclusion. Ask where you heard it, whether the source was reliable, and whether you have ever seen actual evidence. Some studies suggest that prompting people to focus on accuracy, or to think about how they know a claim is true, reduces the effect, especially for claims they have the knowledge to check. It also helps to be careful about what you repeat yourself. Every time you pass on a dubious claim, even as a joke, you add one more exposure to someone else's sense that it's true."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the 1977 study find about statements that were repeated across sessions?", ["They were rated as more likely to be true", "They were rated as less trustworthy", "They were remembered but believed less", "They only gained belief if they were actually true"], 0, "Repeated statements were rated as increasingly likely to be true, whether they were true or false. Only their familiarity had changed."],
    [1, "What is ‘processing fluency’?", ["A measure of how fast someone reads", "The ease with which the brain handles information, which can feel like truth", "A technique for memorising facts", "Being fluent in a second language"], 1, "Familiar or easy-to-process information feels smooth, and the mind tends to treat that ease as a sign the information is accurate."],
    [2, "In Fazio's 2015 study, what happened when people repeatedly read false statements that contradicted their own knowledge?", ["They rejected them completely", "They became sceptical of everything", "Repetition still made the statements seem somewhat more true", "They permanently forgot the correct fact"], 2, "Knowledge helped but didn't switch the effect off. People often relied on the feeling of familiarity instead of retrieving what they knew."],
    [4, "What is the ‘truth sandwich’ approach to correcting a myth?", ["Repeat the myth several times so people notice it", "Ignore the myth completely", "Mock the people who believe it", "Start and finish with the fact, mentioning the myth once in between"], 3, "Leading and ending with the accurate fact means the truth, not the myth, gets the repetition that builds familiarity."],
    [3, "What did Pennycook and colleagues find about fake headlines that carried a ‘disputed’ label?", ["The label made people distrust them permanently", "One earlier exposure still made them seem more accurate later", "People refused to read them", "The label made them twice as believable"], 1, "Even a single prior exposure raised perceived accuracy, and the effect appeared even when headlines had been flagged as disputed by fact-checkers."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("wason-confirmation-bias", "thinking", "beginner", "Confirmation bias: the puzzle almost everyone gets wrong", "Three numbers, one hidden rule, and a mistake that reveals how we all hunt for proof we're right rather than for the truth.",
  [
    ["The 2-4-6 puzzle", "In 1960 Peter Wason gave people a simple number puzzle that exposed a deep habit of thought.",
      "In 1960 the British psychologist Peter Wason, at University College London, gave volunteers three numbers: 2, 4, 6. He told them the sequence followed a rule he had in mind, and their job was to discover it. They could propose their own sets of three numbers, and he would say whether each one fitted the rule. When they felt sure, they could announce their answer. Most people quickly formed a theory, such as ‘even numbers going up by two’, and then tested it with sets like 8, 10, 12 and 20, 22, 24. Every one of those guesses got a yes. Confident, they announced their rule, and many of them were told they were wrong."],
    ["Testing to confirm, not to disprove", "People tested examples that fitted their theory, so they never saw it fail.",
      "Wason's actual rule was simply ‘any three numbers in increasing order’. Sets like 1, 2, 3 or 5, 17, 300 would also have earned a yes. The volunteers who failed had not been careless. They had done something very natural: they looked for examples that fitted their idea, rather than examples that might break it. A test like 8, 10, 12 could never show them they were wrong, because it fitted both their theory and the real rule. The people who succeeded tended to try sequences that their own theory said should fail, such as 1, 2, 3 or 3, 10, 50. When one of those came back with a yes, they learnt something important: their theory was too narrow."],
    ["Four cards on a table", "In Wason's 1966 selection task, most people turn over the wrong cards.",
      "Wason's most famous puzzle followed in 1966. Imagine four cards on a table, each with a letter on one side and a number on the other. You can see E, K, 4 and 7. The rule is: ‘If a card has a vowel on one side, it has an even number on the other.’ Which cards must you turn over to check whether the rule is being broken? Most people pick E, and often 4 as well. E is right. But 4 tells you nothing, because the rule says nothing about what must be behind an even number. The card you need is 7: if a vowel is hiding behind it, the rule is broken. In many studies, only around one person in ten, or fewer, chooses correctly."],
    ["Easy when it's about cheating", "The same logic becomes simple when the puzzle is about catching rule-breakers.",
      "Here's the strange part. Researchers later rewrote the puzzle in familiar terms. Imagine you're checking a bar where the rule is: ‘If someone is drinking beer, they must be over 18.’ The four cards show: drinking beer, drinking cola, age 25 and age 16. Which do you check? Almost everyone instantly picks the beer drinker and the 16-year-old, which is logically the same answer as E and 7. Studies by Richard Griggs and James Cox in 1982 found that versions like this are solved far more often than the abstract one. Psychologists still debate why. Some argue our minds are tuned to spot social cheating; others say familiar situations simply make it obvious which cases could break the rule."],
    ["Confirmation bias everywhere", "We tend to seek, notice and remember information that supports what we already believe.",
      "Wason's puzzles became classic demonstrations of what is now called confirmation bias: the tendency to search for, interpret and remember information in ways that support what we already think. It shows up far beyond the laboratory. A detective who settles on a suspect early may give extra weight to clues that point their way. Someone convinced a colleague dislikes them reads every short email as proof. Investors can seek out news that flatters the shares they already own. Online, search engines and feeds make it easy to find something that agrees with almost any belief. The bias isn't stupidity. Holding on to a working theory saves effort, and often the theory is right. The danger is that we rarely notice when it isn't."],
    ["Try to break your own idea", "The best test of a belief is one that could prove it wrong.",
      "The lesson from Wason is practical. When you have a theory, ask: what would I expect to see if I were wrong, and have I looked for it? Scientists build this into their method by designing experiments that could fail. You can do a small version in daily life. Before a big purchase, read the worst reviews, not just the best. Before deciding someone is untrustworthy, list the evidence that points the other way. In a 1984 study, Charles Lord and colleagues found that asking people to deliberately consider the opposite reduced biased judgements more effectively than simply telling them to be fair and unbiased. Testing your ideas like this feels uncomfortable, but it's the quickest route to the truth."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "What was the real rule in Wason's 2-4-6 task?", ["Even numbers increasing by two", "Numbers that add up to 12", "Any three numbers in increasing order", "Any three even numbers"], 2, "The rule was simply any ascending sequence. People rarely discovered it because they only tested sets that fitted their narrower theory."],
    [1, "Why did testing a set like 8, 10, 12 fail to help people?", ["It fitted both their theory and the real rule, so it couldn't show them they were wrong", "The numbers were too large", "Wason gave misleading answers", "It broke the rule"], 0, "A test that fits every theory you're considering can't tell them apart. Informative tests are ones your theory predicts should fail."],
    [2, "In the card task showing E, K, 4 and 7, which cards must be turned over to check the rule?", ["E and 4", "4 and 7", "K and 4", "E and 7"], 3, "E could have an odd number behind it, and 7 could have a vowel behind it. Either would break the rule. The 4 card can't break it, whatever is on the back."],
    [3, "Why is the drinking-age version of the card puzzle so interesting?", ["It uses a completely different kind of logic", "It has the same logical structure, yet people solve it far more easily", "Nobody can solve it", "It proves people are better at maths when relaxed"], 1, "Checking the beer drinker and the 16-year-old is logically identical to checking E and 7, but the familiar social setting makes the answer feel obvious."],
    [5, "According to the 1984 study by Lord and colleagues, what helped reduce biased judgements?", ["Being told to be fair", "Reading more evidence that supports your view", "Deliberately considering the opposite", "Deciding more quickly"], 2, "Actively imagining how the evidence could point the other way worked better than a general instruction to be unbiased."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("outcome-bias", "thinking", "beginner", "Outcome bias: why good decisions can end badly", "A coach made one of the most criticised calls in sport, and a poker player says we judged it all wrong. Learn to tell a smart choice from a lucky one.",
  [
    ["The worst call ever?", "When a decision ends in disaster, we tend to assume the decision itself was foolish.",
      "In February 2015 the Seattle Seahawks were seconds away from winning the Super Bowl, the biggest game in American football. Trailing the New England Patriots by four points, they had the ball about a metre from the line with 26 seconds left. Most fans expected them to hand it to their powerful running back. Instead, coach Pete Carroll called a pass. It was intercepted by Malcolm Butler, and the Patriots won. Headlines called it one of the worst play calls in the history of the game. Yet a number of analysts argued the call was reasonable: interceptions that close to the line are rare, and an incomplete pass would stop the clock and leave time for more attempts. Had it worked, it might have been praised as clever."],
    ["Resulting", "Judging a decision only by its result is a mistake poker players call ‘resulting’.",
      "The former professional poker player Annie Duke tells the Carroll story in her 2018 book Thinking in Bets. She describes the habit of judging decisions purely by how they turned out using a term from poker: ‘resulting’. In poker it's easy to see the problem. You can play a hand perfectly and still lose because the last card goes against you. You can also make a reckless bet and win. Over thousands of hands, good decisions come out ahead, but any single hand is heavily shaped by luck. Real life works the same way, only with fewer hands and messier information, which makes it much harder to see the difference between a smart choice and a fortunate one."],
    ["The surgeon experiment", "People rate the very same decision as worse when it happens to turn out badly.",
      "In 1988 the psychologists Jonathan Baron and John Hershey gave volunteers descriptions of decisions made under uncertainty, such as a doctor's choice about whether to go ahead with a risky operation. Everyone received the same information that had been available to the decision-maker at the time. The only thing that changed was the outcome: in some versions the patient lived, and in others the patient died. People rated the decision itself as worse, and the decision-maker as less competent, when the outcome was bad. Yet the choice had been made with exactly the same knowledge, and the outcome added nothing to what the doctor could have known beforehand. Baron and Hershey called this outcome bias."],
    ["Four kinds of result", "Every outcome mixes decision quality with luck, giving four very different combinations.",
      "A helpful way to picture this is a simple grid. A good decision with a good outcome is a deserved success. A good decision with a bad outcome is bad luck. A bad decision with a bad outcome is a predictable failure. And a bad decision with a good outcome is a lucky escape, which may be the most dangerous of all. Think of someone who drives home after too many drinks and arrives safely. The outcome teaches them nothing useful, or worse, teaches them that it was fine. Each lucky escape can make the next risky choice feel safer. Outcome bias collapses these four boxes into two: it worked, so it was right; it failed, so it was wrong."],
    ["Hindsight makes it worse", "Once we know the result, we overestimate how predictable it was.",
      "Outcome bias has a close relative: hindsight bias, the feeling that we ‘knew it all along’. In the 1970s the psychologist Baruch Fischhoff showed that once people learnt how an event turned out, they judged that outcome to have been more predictable than people who hadn't been told. Their memories of what they had predicted earlier also shifted towards what actually happened. Together these biases make the world seem more orderly than it really is. They also make us unfair judges. Workers, doctors, pilots and leaders can be blamed for bad outcomes they could not reasonably have foreseen, while people who took foolish risks and got away with them can be rewarded and promoted."],
    ["Judge the process", "To learn from experience, review how a decision was made, not just how it turned out.",
      "The fix is to judge decisions by the process behind them. Ask: what did we know at the time? What options did we consider? Did we think about the risks and how likely they were? Was there information we could reasonably have gathered but didn't? Armies, hospitals and airlines have long used structured reviews after events, and these work best when they examine successes and failures with the same honesty. A simple personal tool is a decision journal. Before an important choice, write down what you expect to happen and why. Later, compare that with what actually happened. Over time you'll see whether your judgement is sound, or whether you've simply been lucky."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do some analysts defend Pete Carroll's 2015 Super Bowl call?", ["The pass was actually completed", "Interceptions that close to the line are rare, and a pass could save time", "The rules required a pass in that situation", "The running back was injured"], 1, "Judged on the information available, the pass had a low chance of interception and helped manage the clock. The bad outcome doesn't by itself prove the decision was bad."],
    [1, "What does ‘resulting’ mean?", ["Judging a decision purely by how it turned out", "Recording the score after each game", "Making decisions very quickly", "Betting only on likely winners"], 0, "Annie Duke borrowed the poker term to describe confusing the quality of a decision with the quality of its outcome."],
    [2, "In Baron and Hershey's study, what changed between versions of each scenario?", ["The information available to the doctor", "The doctor's experience", "Only the outcome", "The type of operation"], 2, "Everything the decision-maker knew stayed the same. Only the result changed, yet people judged the decision differently."],
    [3, "Why might a bad decision with a good outcome be especially dangerous?", ["It always leads to punishment", "It can teach people that a risky choice was fine", "It is impossible to notice", "It makes people far too cautious"], 1, "A lucky escape rewards the risky behaviour, making it feel safer and more likely to be repeated."],
    [4, "What is hindsight bias?", ["Forgetting decisions you made", "Planning too far ahead", "Trusting experts after a mistake", "Seeing an outcome as more predictable once you know it"], 3, "Fischhoff showed that knowing the result makes it seem more foreseeable, and even shifts our memories of what we predicted."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("n-rays-blondlot", "thinking", "beginner", "N-rays: the rays only believers could see", "In 1903 a respected French physicist discovered a new kind of radiation, and other scientists soon confirmed it. There was just one problem: it didn't exist.",
  [
    ["An age of invisible rays", "After the discovery of X-rays, physicists were primed to find new kinds of radiation.",
      "At the end of the 1800s, physics was full of astonishing discoveries. In 1895 Wilhelm Röntgen found X-rays, which could pass through flesh and reveal the bones beneath. The next year Henri Becquerel discovered that uranium gave off its own mysterious radiation, and soon Marie and Pierre Curie were isolating new radioactive elements. Invisible rays seemed to be everywhere, waiting to be found. In 1903 René Blondlot, a highly respected physicist at the University of Nancy in France, announced that he had found another kind. He named them N-rays, after his home city. Given everything that had happened in the previous eight years, the claim did not seem outlandish. It seemed like the next exciting chapter."],
    ["Watching for a faint glow", "N-rays were detected by tiny changes in brightness that observers judged by eye.",
      "Blondlot reported that N-rays were given off by many sources, including certain lamps and heated metals, and some colleagues soon claimed they came from the human body as well. The key detail was how they were detected. N-rays supposedly made a small electric spark or a faintly glowing screen grow slightly brighter. Observers sat in a darkened room and judged these changes with their own eyes. That is a very weak signal to rely on. In dim light, the human eye struggles to judge small differences in brightness, and the tiny flickers that happen anyway are easy to read as meaningful. Even so, other scientists, many of them French, soon reported observations of their own, and a stream of research papers followed."],
    ["Others couldn't see them", "Skilled laboratories outside France tried and failed to reproduce the effect.",
      "Not everyone was convinced. Physicists in Germany and Britain, among them some of the most skilled experimenters of the day, set up the apparatus and saw nothing. Supporters had an answer ready: perhaps the sceptics' eyes were not sensitive enough, or they lacked the right technique. This is a warning sign worth remembering. When a result can only be seen by people who already believe in it, and failures are explained away as the observer's fault, the claim has become very difficult to test. A real effect should show up for careful researchers whether or not they hope to find it. The disagreement rumbled on, until a visiting American physicist decided to investigate for himself."],
    ["The missing prism", "In 1904 Robert Wood secretly removed a vital part of the equipment, and the readings carried on.",
      "In 1904 the American physicist Robert W. Wood, of Johns Hopkins University, visited Blondlot's laboratory. Blondlot demonstrated how an aluminium prism supposedly spread N-rays out into a spectrum, with readings taken in a darkened room. While the room was dark, Wood quietly removed the prism and kept it out of sight. Blondlot carried on reading out measurements as if nothing had changed. Wood also reported other secret checks in which observers kept seeing effects that should have vanished. He published his account in the scientific journal Nature later that year. Without accusing anyone of dishonesty, the report made it clear that the observations depended on what the observers expected, not on any real rays."],
    ["Not fraud, but self-deception", "Blondlot almost certainly believed what he saw; his perception was guided by his expectations.",
      "There is no good evidence that Blondlot set out to deceive anyone. He was an accomplished scientist who had done respected earlier work measuring the speed of electrical waves. What went wrong was more ordinary and more unsettling: he and others genuinely perceived what they expected to perceive. After Wood's report, interest in N-rays faded quickly, especially outside France, though Blondlot is said to have kept believing in them. The episode is now a classic case study in how expectation shapes observation. The chemist Irving Langmuir later popularised the phrase ‘pathological science’ for cases like this, where researchers fool themselves with effects so faint they sit right at the edge of what can be detected."],
    ["Clever Hans and the cure: blinding", "Good science guards against expectation by keeping observers from knowing what to expect.",
      "A similar lesson had come from a horse. In early 1900s Berlin, a horse called Clever Hans appeared to answer arithmetic questions by tapping his hoof. In 1907 the psychologist Oskar Pfungst showed that Hans was responding to tiny, unconscious cues from the people around him, who tensed and relaxed as he neared the right answer. When the questioner didn't know the answer, or Hans couldn't see them, his skill vanished. The solution to both stories is blinding. In a well-run modern trial, the people measuring results don't know which participants received the treatment. Wood, in effect, ran a blind test. Whenever you can, test a claim in a way that stops the tester's hopes from steering the result."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did N-rays seem believable in 1903?", ["They had been predicted decades earlier", "They were bright enough to see in daylight", "X-rays and radioactivity had recently been discovered", "Every laboratory in Europe had detected them"], 2, "Coming just after X-rays and radioactivity, another invisible ray seemed like a natural next discovery."],
    [1, "How were N-rays supposedly detected?", ["With a Geiger counter", "By slight brightening of a spark or glowing screen, judged by eye", "By the heat they produced", "By the sound they made in a vacuum tube"], 1, "Observers judged faint changes in brightness in a dark room, a signal easily shaped by expectation and random flicker."],
    [2, "What warning sign appeared when other laboratories couldn't find N-rays?", ["Failures were blamed on the sceptics' eyes or technique", "The sceptics refused to try the experiment", "The equipment was too expensive to build", "The sceptics were not trained physicists"], 0, "When a claim's failures are always explained away as the observer's fault, it becomes almost impossible to test."],
    [3, "What did Robert Wood do during his 1904 visit?", ["Bribed the laboratory assistants", "Proved N-rays came from the sun", "Secretly removed the prism, yet the readings carried on", "Measured the rays with better instruments"], 2, "Removing the essential prism should have changed the results. When it didn't, it showed the readings came from expectation."],
    [5, "What does ‘blinding’ mean in an experiment?", ["Running it in complete darkness", "Keeping observers unaware of information that could sway their judgement", "Hiding the results from the public", "Testing only on animals"], 1, "Blinding stops the hopes and expectations of the people involved from influencing what they see or record."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("deduction-induction-abduction", "thinking", "beginner", "Deduction, induction and abduction: three ways to reason", "Sherlock Holmes called it deduction, but he was usually doing something else. Learn the three basic kinds of reasoning, and what each can and can't prove.",
  [
    ["‘You have been in Afghanistan, I perceive’", "Holmes's first meeting with Watson shows reasoning from small clues to a big conclusion.",
      "In A Study in Scarlet, published in 1887, Sherlock Holmes shakes hands with Dr John Watson and immediately says, ‘You have been in Afghanistan, I perceive.’ Watson is astonished. Later Holmes explains his chain of thought. Here was a gentleman of a medical type with a military air, so probably an army doctor. His face was tanned but his wrists were pale, so he had recently come from the tropics. He looked haggard and held his left arm stiffly, suggesting illness and an injury. Where might an English army doctor recently have suffered such hardship? Afghanistan. Holmes called this the science of deduction. Logicians, though, would say he was mostly doing something different. To see why, we need three kinds of reasoning."],
    ["Deduction: guaranteed conclusions", "In a valid deduction, if the premises are true, the conclusion must be true.",
      "Deduction moves from general statements to a conclusion that follows necessarily. The classic example: all humans are mortal; Socrates is human; therefore Socrates is mortal. If both starting statements, called premises, are true, the conclusion cannot be false. That is deduction's great strength: certainty. Its weakness is that the conclusion contains nothing that wasn't already packed inside the premises. Deduction also depends completely on the premises being right. An argument can be perfectly valid in its structure and still reach a false conclusion if a premise is wrong: all birds can fly; penguins are birds; therefore penguins can fly. Logicians call an argument ‘sound’ only when it is valid and its premises are actually true."],
    ["Induction: learning from patterns", "Induction generalises from experience, which is useful but never completely certain.",
      "Induction works the other way, from particular observations to general conclusions. The sun has risen every morning in recorded history, so it will rise tomorrow. Every swan Europeans had ever seen was white, so all swans must be white. Most of our everyday knowledge, and much of science, rests on reasoning like this, and usually it serves us well. But an inductive conclusion can always be overturned by new evidence. In 1697 the Dutch explorer Willem de Vlamingh and his crew saw black swans on a river in what is now Western Australia, which became known as the Swan River. A belief backed by countless observations was undone by a single flock of birds."],
    ["Abduction: the best explanation", "Abduction reasons from evidence to the most likely explanation, as detectives and doctors do.",
      "The third kind of reasoning was named abduction by the American philosopher Charles Sanders Peirce in the late 1800s. It starts with a surprising observation and asks what would best explain it. The lawn is wet this morning. Did it rain, did the sprinkler run, or did dew form overnight? You weigh up the clues, perhaps noticing that the footpath is bone dry, which points to the sprinkler. Abduction is also called inference to the best explanation. This is what Holmes was really doing with Watson: choosing the most plausible story to fit the clues. Doctors do the same when they diagnose an illness from symptoms, and mechanics do it when tracing a strange noise in an engine."],
    ["Where each one goes wrong", "Abduction can miss alternatives, and a common logical slip treats it like deduction.",
      "Abduction is powerful but risky, because the best explanation you can think of may not be the true one. Perhaps Watson was tanned from a holiday and had hurt his arm falling off a horse. The answer is only as good as the range of explanations you consider. A common error is to treat abduction as if it were deduction. ‘If it rained, the lawn would be wet. The lawn is wet. Therefore it rained.’ That feels logical, but it's a known fallacy called affirming the consequent, because other things could have wet the lawn. Induction has its own trap: generalising from too few cases, such as judging a whole city on one rude waiter."],
    ["Using all three together", "Good thinking cycles through explanation, prediction and testing.",
      "In practice the three kinds of reasoning work as a team, and science is a good model. A researcher notices something odd and uses abduction to propose a likely explanation, called a hypothesis. They then use deduction to work out what should happen if that explanation is true: if this fertiliser helps plants grow, then plants given it should grow taller than identical plants without it. Finally they gather observations and use induction to judge whether the evidence supports the idea. Then the cycle starts again. You can do the same with everyday puzzles. When you land on an explanation, ask what else could explain the facts, what your idea predicts, and whether you've checked."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which kind of reasoning was Holmes mostly using when he guessed Watson had been in Afghanistan?", ["Deduction", "Abduction", "Induction", "Pure intuition"], 1, "Holmes was inferring the most plausible explanation for a set of clues, which is abduction, not a guaranteed deduction."],
    [1, "What makes a deductive argument ‘sound’?", ["It is convincing to most people", "It is based on many observations", "It is valid and its premises are true", "It has a surprising conclusion"], 2, "A valid argument with a false premise can reach a false conclusion. Soundness requires both correct structure and true premises."],
    [2, "What did the black swans seen in 1697 show about induction?", ["A conclusion based on many observations can be overturned by one new one", "Induction is always wrong", "Swans migrate to Australia each year", "Deduction is more reliable than observation"], 0, "Countless sightings of white swans didn't prove all swans were white. One new observation was enough to overturn the generalisation."],
    [4, "‘If it rained, the lawn would be wet. The lawn is wet, so it rained.’ What's wrong with this?", ["Nothing, it is a valid deduction", "It assumes rain is rare", "It relies on too few observations", "It affirms the consequent, ignoring other possible causes"], 3, "A wet lawn fits rain, but also a sprinkler or dew. Treating a possible explanation as certain is the fallacy of affirming the consequent."],
    [5, "In the scientific cycle, what role does deduction usually play?", ["Working out what should happen if a hypothesis is true", "Coming up with the first guess", "Collecting observations", "Replacing the need for evidence"], 0, "Deduction turns a hypothesis into testable predictions, which observations can then support or undermine."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("framing-effect", "thinking", "intermediate", "The framing effect: same facts, different choices", "Would you choose a treatment with a 90 per cent survival rate? What about one with a 10 per cent death rate? They're identical, yet the wording changes what people choose.",
  [
    ["An imaginary outbreak", "Describing the same options as lives saved or lives lost flipped people's choices.",
      "In 1981 the psychologists Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman published a now-famous problem. Imagine a disease outbreak expected to kill 600 people. One group of participants chose between two programs. With Program A, 200 people will be saved. With Program B, there's a one-third chance all 600 are saved and a two-thirds chance no-one is saved. Most chose A, the sure thing. A second group got the same choice in different words. With Program C, 400 people will die. With Program D, there's a one-third chance nobody dies and a two-thirds chance all 600 die. Now most chose D, the gamble. Yet A and C describe exactly the same outcome, as do B and D."],
    ["Gains feel safe, losses invite a gamble", "People tend to avoid risk when thinking about gains, and take risks to avoid sure losses.",
      "The outbreak problem revealed a pattern that Kahneman and Tversky built into their prospect theory. When outcomes are described as gains, such as lives saved, people tend to be cautious and prefer a certain result. When the same outcomes are described as losses, such as deaths, people become more willing to gamble to avoid a sure loss. Part of the reason is loss aversion: losses tend to feel more painful than equivalent gains feel good, with many studies putting the ratio at roughly two to one, though estimates vary. Logically, the wording should make no difference, because the numbers are identical. Psychologically, the frame sets the reference point against which everything else is judged."],
    ["Doctors are not immune", "Even physicians' choices shifted depending on whether outcomes were framed as survival or death.",
      "You might hope experts would see through framing. In a 1982 study published in the New England Journal of Medicine, Barbara McNeil and colleagues, including Tversky, asked people to choose between surgery and radiation therapy for lung cancer. Surgery carried a higher risk of dying during or soon after treatment, but better long-term survival. When the results were presented as chances of surviving, surgery looked more attractive than when the very same results were described as chances of dying. The effect appeared among patients, among students, and among physicians. Since then, many guides to communicating medical risk have encouraged presenting figures in more than one way, for example giving both survival and death rates together."],
    ["75 per cent lean", "Labels that highlight the positive side make products seem better, even after tasting them.",
      "Framing is everywhere in shopping. In a 1988 study, Irwin Levin and Gary Gaeth asked people to evaluate minced beef described either as ‘75 per cent lean’ or ‘25 per cent fat’. It was the same meat, but people rated the ‘lean’ version as better quality and less greasy. Interestingly, the gap shrank when people actually tasted the beef, though it didn't vanish. Marketers know this well. Yoghurt is ‘95 per cent fat free’ rather than ‘5 per cent fat’. A fee might be presented as a ‘discount for paying cash’ instead of a ‘surcharge for paying by card’. None of these descriptions is false. They simply choose which side of the same coin you see first."],
    ["Framing the question", "In politics and news, choosing which aspect of an issue to emphasise can shift opinions.",
      "There's a second, broader kind of framing. Instead of describing the same numbers in two ways, it highlights different aspects of an issue. In a 1997 study by Thomas Nelson, Rosalee Clawson and Zoe Oxley, Americans read a news story about a planned rally by the Ku Klux Klan. When the story framed it as a matter of free speech, readers were more tolerant of the rally going ahead than when the same event was framed as a threat to public order. Debates about tax, immigration, crime and climate are full of these frames. Is a policy ‘tax relief’ or a ‘cut to services’? Each phrase invites you to think about one side of the trade-off and quietly forget the other."],
    ["Defusing the frame", "Restate choices in more than one way and convert percentages into real numbers before deciding.",
      "You can't avoid frames, because every description has to start somewhere. But you can learn to notice them. When you face an important choice, deliberately flip the wording: if you're told nine out of ten customers are satisfied, remind yourself that one in ten isn't. If a deal is framed as avoiding a loss, ask how it looks as a plain gain. Turn percentages into concrete counts, such as ‘out of 100 people like me, how many?’ This makes risks much easier to compare. Be especially wary when someone else has chosen the frame for you and stands to gain from your decision. And when you present information to others, giving both frames is often the fairest thing you can do."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "In the outbreak problem, why is the switch in people's choices surprising?", ["The programs had genuinely different outcomes", "Programs A and C described exactly the same outcome", "The second group was given more information", "The participants were all doctors"], 1, "Saving 200 of 600 is the same as 400 dying. Only the wording changed, yet the majority preference reversed."],
    [1, "When outcomes are framed as losses, people tend to:", ["Become more cautious", "Choose whichever option is listed first", "Become more willing to gamble", "Ignore the numbers entirely"], 2, "Facing a sure loss feels so painful that people often prefer a risky option that might avoid it altogether."],
    [2, "Who was influenced by framing in the 1982 lung cancer study?", ["Only students", "Only patients without medical training", "Nobody, once the numbers were explained", "Patients, students and physicians alike"], 3, "Physicians were affected too, which is why presenting risks in more than one frame is often recommended."],
    [3, "What happened in the minced beef study when people actually tasted the meat?", ["The framing effect shrank but didn't disappear", "The effect became much stronger", "People preferred the ‘fat’ label", "Tasting made them reject both samples"], 0, "Direct experience reduced the influence of the label, but the ‘lean’ description still gave the beef an edge."],
    [5, "Which is a good way to defuse a frame?", ["Go with your first reaction", "Restate the choice the other way and convert percentages into counts", "Only trust positive descriptions", "Avoid numbers completely"], 1, "Seeing both versions and using concrete counts strips away the emotional pull of a single framing."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("wisdom-of-crowds", "thinking", "intermediate", "The wisdom of crowds: when many guesses beat experts", "In 1906 hundreds of people guessed the weight of an ox. Hardly anyone was spot on, but together they were astonishingly close. Here's why, and when crowds turn foolish.",
  [
    ["The ox at Plymouth", "Francis Galton found that a crowd's middle guess was remarkably close to an ox's true weight.",
      "In 1906 the scientist Francis Galton visited a livestock fair in Plymouth, England. Visitors could pay a small fee to guess the weight of an ox once it had been slaughtered and dressed, with prizes for the closest entries. Galton, who was not a great admirer of ordinary people's judgement, collected the 787 legible tickets afterwards and analysed them. The guesses were all over the place. But the middle value, the median, was 1,207 pounds, about 547 kilograms. The actual weight was 1,198 pounds, so the crowd was out by less than one per cent. Later analyses found the average of the guesses was even closer. Galton published the result in Nature in 1907 under the title ‘Vox Populi’, the voice of the people."],
    ["Why errors cancel out", "When people's mistakes are independent, overestimates and underestimates tend to cancel each other out.",
      "How can a crowd of non-experts beat almost every individual in it? Each guess contains two parts: some genuine information and some error. One person might overestimate because the ox looks bulky, while another underestimates because they're thinking of a smaller breed. If those errors point in different directions, they tend to cancel out when you average them, while the useful information adds up. The result is an estimate that is often better than most of the individual guesses, and sometimes better than an expert's. But this only works if people bring different knowledge and make their mistakes independently. If everyone shares the same blind spot, averaging simply produces a confident version of the same error."],
    ["Ask the audience", "Combining many judgements works in plenty of everyday settings, from quiz shows to forecasts.",
      "The idea was popularised by the journalist James Surowiecki in his 2004 book The Wisdom of Crowds. One of his examples came from the quiz show Who Wants to Be a Millionaire? Contestants who were stuck could phone a friend they had chosen for being smart, or poll the studio audience. By Surowiecki's account, the chosen friends gave the right answer about 65 per cent of the time, while the audience's most popular answer was right about 91 per cent of the time. Similar principles underpin many tools today, from averaging the forecasts of several economists to combining many runs of weather models, and the product ratings that pool the experiences of thousands of buyers."],
    ["Four conditions", "Crowds are wise when they are diverse, independent, decentralised and properly combined.",
      "Surowiecki argued that wise crowds need four ingredients. Diversity: people should bring different information and perspectives, not all the same views. Independence: each person's judgement should be their own, rather than copied from those around them. Decentralisation: people should be able to draw on local and specialised knowledge. And aggregation: there must be some way of combining all the private judgements into one answer, such as an average, a vote or a market price. Take away any of these and the magic fades. A room of people who all read the same article, or who call out their guesses one after another, is less a crowd of independent minds than one opinion repeated many times."],
    ["When crowds go wrong", "Social influence can make crowds more confident without making them more accurate.",
      "Independence is the fragile ingredient. In a 2011 study, Jan Lorenz and colleagues in Switzerland asked groups to estimate factual quantities, such as crime figures, over several rounds. Some groups were shown what the others had guessed before answering again. Seeing others' answers narrowed the range of guesses and made people more confident, but the group's estimates did not become more accurate. The crowd had lost its diversity without gaining any wisdom. On a larger scale, similar dynamics help explain stock market bubbles, fads and panic buying, where people react to each other rather than to independent information. A crowd that copies itself doesn't cancel out its errors. It can amplify them."],
    ["The crowd inside your head", "Averaging two separate guesses of your own can improve your accuracy.",
      "You can even borrow the effect for yourself. In 2008 Edward Vul and Harold Pashler asked people to answer factual questions, then unexpectedly asked them to guess again. The average of a person's two guesses was more accurate than either guess alone, and the benefit was larger when the second guess came three weeks later rather than straight away. The delay helped because it made the second guess more independent of the first. Researchers call this the crowd within. A practical version: when you estimate something important, such as how long a job will take, make a guess, then assume your first answer was wrong, think about why, and make a second estimate. Then average the two."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was striking about the guesses at the Plymouth fair?", ["Most people guessed exactly right", "The median guess was within about one per cent of the true weight", "The butchers' guesses were by far the best", "Nobody guessed within 100 pounds"], 1, "Individual guesses varied widely, but the middle value of all 787 was only nine pounds off the actual weight."],
    [1, "Why does averaging many guesses work?", ["Independent errors tend to cancel out", "Crowds always contain an expert", "People guess more carefully in groups", "Large numbers are easier to estimate"], 0, "When errors point in different directions, they offset each other, leaving the shared genuine information."],
    [3, "Which of these is NOT one of Surowiecki's conditions for a wise crowd?", ["Diversity", "Independence", "Aggregation", "Unanimity"], 3, "Wise crowds need diversity, independence, decentralisation and aggregation. Everyone agreeing is not required, and can be a warning sign."],
    [4, "What did Lorenz and colleagues find when people saw others' guesses?", ["Accuracy improved sharply", "Guesses spread out more widely", "Guesses narrowed and confidence rose, without the group becoming more accurate", "People stopped guessing altogether"], 2, "Social influence reduced diversity and boosted confidence, but didn't improve the collective estimate."],
    [5, "In Vul and Pashler's study, when did a second guess help most?", ["When it was made immediately", "When it was made three weeks later", "When a friend made it instead", "When it copied the first guess"], 1, "A delay made the second guess more independent of the first, so averaging the two cancelled out more error."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("law-of-small-numbers", "thinking", "intermediate", "Small samples, big surprises: the law of small numbers", "The places with the lowest cancer rates and the highest ones share the same surprising feature. Understanding why will change how you read every ranking and review.",
  [
    ["A puzzle about cancer maps", "Both the healthiest and unhealthiest-looking places on a map are often the ones with the fewest people.",
      "Imagine a map of the United States showing kidney cancer rates county by county. The counties with the lowest rates are mostly rural and sparsely populated. It's tempting to explain this: fresh air, clean water, active lives, fewer pollutants. Now look at the counties with the highest rates. They are also mostly rural and sparsely populated. Suddenly the explanations sound less convincing, since rural life can't be both protective and harmful. This example, discussed by the statistician Howard Wainer and later by Daniel Kahneman in his book Thinking, Fast and Slow, has a simple answer. Small counties don't have unusual health. They have small populations, and small numbers bounce around far more than large ones."],
    ["Why small samples swing wildly", "The smaller the sample, the more likely it is to produce extreme results by chance.",
      "Think of flipping coins. Toss a fair coin four times and getting all heads is not unusual: it happens about one time in sixteen. Toss it 400 times and getting all heads is practically impossible. Results from small samples are naturally more variable, so they are much more likely to land at the extremes, both very high and very low. A county with a few thousand residents might record no cases of a rare cancer for years, then an unusual cluster, purely by chance. A city of a million people will almost always land close to the national average. So whenever you rank places, schools or hospitals of very different sizes, the small ones will tend to crowd both the top and the bottom of the list."],
    ["The hospital question", "Even people trained in statistics tend to ignore sample size when it matters.",
      "In the early 1970s Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman posed a question. A town has two hospitals. In the larger one about 45 babies are born each day, and in the smaller one about 15. Roughly half of all babies are boys, but the exact share varies from day to day. Over a year, each hospital records the days when more than 60 per cent of the babies born were boys. Which hospital recorded more such days? Most people said about the same. The right answer is the smaller hospital, because with only 15 births a day, lopsided results are much more common. In an earlier paper titled ‘Belief in the law of small numbers’, they had shown that even research psychologists trusted small samples too much."],
    ["The most dangerous equation", "Uncertainty in an average shrinks only with the square root of the sample size.",
      "The maths behind this traces back to the 1700s and the French-born mathematician Abraham de Moivre. In modern terms, the typical wobble in an average shrinks in proportion to the square root of the number of things being averaged. That has a sobering consequence. To halve the uncertainty in an estimate, you don't need twice as much data; you need four times as much. To cut it to a tenth, you need a hundred times more. Howard Wainer has called this ‘the most dangerous equation’, not because it's complicated, but because ignoring it has led to so many costly mistakes. It explains why tiny trials, small surveys and short track records can be so misleading."],
    ["The small schools mistake", "Policies based on small-sample rankings can end up chasing patterns that are really noise.",
      "Wainer's favourite cautionary tale involves American education. From the late 1990s, reformers noticed that small schools were overrepresented among the top performers on test results. Major funders, including the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation, invested heavily in creating smaller schools and breaking up big ones. But as Wainer and colleagues pointed out, small schools were also overrepresented among the worst performers. Their results were simply more variable, because each year's scores came from fewer students. Evaluations of the small-schools push produced mixed results, and the Gates Foundation later shifted its education strategy. Whatever the other merits of smaller schools, the striking rankings had been at least partly an illusion created by sample size."],
    ["Reading rankings wisely", "Before trusting an extreme result, check how many cases it's based on.",
      "Once you know this, you'll see it everywhere. A new café with five reviews averaging five stars may be no better than an established one averaging 4.6 from 2,000 reviews. A footballer who scores in each of their first three games isn't necessarily a star. A small study reporting a dramatic effect is more likely to be a fluke than a large study reporting a modest one. A useful habit is to mentally pull extreme results from small samples back towards the average until more evidence comes in. Statisticians have formal versions of this idea, sometimes called shrinkage. And when you hear that a small town is the best or worst place for something, your first question should be: how many people live there?"]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What do US counties with the lowest and the highest kidney cancer rates often have in common?", ["They have small populations", "They are close to large cities", "They share the same water supply", "They are all on the coast"], 0, "Small populations produce more variable rates, so they land at both extremes by chance."],
    [2, "In the hospital problem, which hospital records more days when over 60 per cent of babies are boys?", ["The larger hospital", "Both about the same", "The smaller hospital", "It depends on the season"], 2, "With only 15 births a day, lopsided results happen far more often than with 45 births a day."],
    [3, "To halve the uncertainty in an average, roughly how much more data do you need?", ["Twice as much", "Four times as much", "Ten times as much", "Half as much"], 1, "Uncertainty shrinks with the square root of sample size, so halving it takes four times the data."],
    [4, "Why were small schools overrepresented among the top performers?", ["Their teachers were always better", "They received more funding", "They set easier tests", "Their results varied more because each was based on fewer students"], 3, "Small schools also crowded the bottom of the rankings. Their extreme results were largely a product of small samples."],
    [5, "A new café has five reviews, all five stars. What's the most sensible view?", ["It's definitely the best café in town", "The rating is uncertain, so mentally pull it back towards average", "Five-star ratings are always fake", "Ignore all reviews from now on"], 1, "With so few reviews, an extreme average is likely to drift as more come in. Treat it as promising, not proven."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("piltdown-man-hoax", "thinking", "intermediate", "Piltdown Man: the fake fossil that fooled science for 40 years", "For four decades, a skull from an English gravel pit was hailed as a key link in human evolution. It was a forgery, and the reasons experts believed it are the real lesson.",
  [
    ["A discovery in Sussex", "In 1912 bones from a Sussex gravel pit were announced as an ancient human ancestor.",
      "In December 1912 Charles Dawson, a solicitor and keen amateur fossil collector, and Arthur Smith Woodward, a leading geologist at the British Museum (Natural History), presented a sensational find to the Geological Society of London. From a gravel pit near Piltdown in Sussex came fragments of a skull and part of a jawbone with two worn teeth, along with animal fossils and crude stone tools. Woodward's reconstruction showed a creature with a large, humanlike braincase and an apelike jaw. It was given the scientific name Eoanthropus dawsoni, ‘Dawson's dawn man’. Newspapers celebrated the find as the earliest Englishman, and many experts accepted Piltdown as a vital link between apes and humans."],
    ["It told experts what they expected", "Piltdown fitted the popular belief that big brains evolved first.",
      "Part of Piltdown's power was that it confirmed what many scientists already thought. A common view at the time was that humans had become human through intelligence, so a large brain must have evolved before other human features, such as upright walking or smaller teeth. Piltdown seemed to show exactly that: a big brain paired with an ape's jaw. National pride may also have played a part. Important fossils had been found in Germany, such as the Neanderthal remains and the Heidelberg jaw, and France had its Cro-Magnon skeletons. Britain had little of comparable importance. A find that matched both scientific expectation and national hopes faced far less scrutiny than it deserved."],
    ["Convenient gaps and limited access", "The parts that could have exposed the fake were missing, and few researchers saw the original bones.",
      "Looking back, there were warning signs. The jaw was broken in exactly the places that would have shown whether it truly belonged with the skull: the joint where jaw meets skull and the chin were both missing. Some scientists, especially in the United States and France, argued from early on that the skull and jaw came from different creatures. But most researchers were given plaster casts rather than access to the original fossils, which were closely guarded at the British Museum. Casts could not show details, such as tool marks or staining, that might have raised suspicion. Later finds, including a canine tooth in 1913 and fragments Dawson said he found at a second site, seemed to settle the debate."],
    ["A real ancestor dismissed", "Because Piltdown seemed authentic, a genuine African fossil was sidelined for years.",
      "The forgery did real damage. In 1924 the Australian-born anatomist Raymond Dart, working in South Africa, identified a small fossil skull from a quarry at Taung. In 1925 he argued in Nature that this ‘Taung Child’ belonged to an early human relative, which he named Australopithecus africanus. It had the opposite combination to Piltdown: a small, apelike brain but humanlike teeth and signs of upright posture. Many leading experts dismissed it, partly because it clashed with Piltdown's big-brain-first story. It took more than two decades, and many further fossil discoveries in Africa, before Dart's view was widely accepted. We now know that upright walking came long before large brains."],
    ["Exposed in 1953", "New dating methods and careful examination proved the bones were a recent fake.",
      "As more genuine fossils emerged, Piltdown looked increasingly out of place. In 1949 Kenneth Oakley applied a new fluorine test, which helps estimate how long bones have been buried by measuring the fluorine they absorb from groundwater. The Piltdown bones contained surprisingly little, suggesting they were far younger than claimed. In 1953 Oakley, together with Joseph Weiner and Wilfrid Le Gros Clark, announced the truth. The jaw came from an orangutan. Its teeth had been filed down to look humanlike. The skull belonged to a modern human who had lived only centuries earlier, and the bones had been stained to look ancient. Under a microscope, the file marks on the teeth were plain to see."],
    ["Who did it?", "Suspicion fell on many people, but a 2016 study points strongly to Dawson.",
      "For decades, people argued over who had planted the fakes. Suspects included Woodward; the Jesuit priest and scientist Pierre Teilhard de Chardin, who found the canine tooth; and even Arthur Conan Doyle, the creator of Sherlock Holmes, who lived nearby. In 2016 a team led by Isabelle De Groote published a detailed re-examination using DNA analysis and CT scanning. The evidence suggested the jaw and teeth from both sites came from a single orangutan, and that the same methods had been used throughout, including packing bones with gravel and putty. That pointed to a single forger, and Dawson, who has also been linked to other dubious finds, was by far the most likely culprit."],
    ["Lessons from a forgery", "Be most careful with evidence that tells you exactly what you hoped to hear.",
      "Piltdown wasn't exposed because science is infallible; it was exposed because science keeps testing. Better methods and new evidence eventually revealed what expectation had hidden. But the story offers lessons well beyond fossils. Be extra careful with evidence that confirms exactly what you, your team or your country hoped to find. Ask whether anyone has examined the original data, rather than a summary or a copy. Notice when crucial pieces of evidence are conveniently missing. And remember that the anomaly that doesn't fit, like the Taung Child, may turn out to be the real discovery. When an idea seems to explain everything you expected, that's a moment to look harder, not to relax."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "Why did Piltdown Man fit what many experts expected?", ["It had a large brain with an apelike jaw, matching the idea that big brains evolved first", "It was found alongside written records", "It looked exactly like a modern human", "It came from Africa, as Darwin had predicted"], 0, "Piltdown seemed to confirm the popular theory that intelligence came first in human evolution, which lowered experts' guard."],
    [2, "Why were most researchers unable to spot the forgery early on?", ["The fossils were lost for decades", "They mostly worked from plaster casts rather than the original bones", "The bones were kept in Africa", "No-one was allowed to publish about them"], 1, "Casts hid the file marks and staining that close examination of the originals would later reveal."],
    [3, "What was Raymond Dart's Taung Child?", ["Another forgery", "A Neanderthal skull", "A genuine early human relative that experts long dismissed", "An orangutan skull"], 2, "The Taung Child was real, but because it contradicted Piltdown's story, it took decades to be widely accepted."],
    [4, "What did Oakley's fluorine test reveal?", ["The bones were millions of years old", "The bones came from Africa", "The skull belonged to an ape", "The bones were far younger than claimed"], 3, "The bones had absorbed too little fluorine to be ancient, the first strong clue that Piltdown was a recent fake."],
    [5, "What did the 2016 re-examination suggest?", ["Arthur Conan Doyle was the forger", "Several forgers worked independently", "A single forger, most likely Dawson, was responsible", "The bones were genuine after all"], 2, "Consistent methods and DNA from a single orangutan pointed to one forger, and Dawson was the most likely candidate."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("lady-tasting-tea", "thinking", "advanced", "The lady tasting tea: how to design a fair test", "A scientist claimed she could taste whether the milk or the tea went into the cup first. Working out how to test her claim properly helped shape the modern experiment.",
  [
    ["A cup of tea at Rothamsted", "A casual claim at afternoon tea inspired Ronald Fisher's thinking about how to test ideas.",
      "In the early 1920s, at the Rothamsted agricultural research station north of London, the statistician Ronald Fisher offered a colleague a cup of tea. Dr Muriel Bristol, a scientist who studied algae, declined because he had poured the milk in after the tea. She preferred milk first, and insisted she could taste the difference. Fisher was sceptical. According to later accounts from people who were there, a test was quickly arranged, and she reportedly identified the cups correctly. The story might have been forgotten, but Fisher used it to open a chapter of his 1935 book The Design of Experiments. Behind a light-hearted example lay a serious question: how do you fairly test a claim?"],
    ["What would luck look like?", "A fair test starts by asking what pure guessing would produce, an idea called the null hypothesis.",
      "Fisher's key move was to start by assuming the claim is false. Suppose Bristol has no ability at all and is simply guessing. This assumption is now called the null hypothesis. If we can work out what results guessing would produce, we can ask whether her actual performance is surprising by comparison. Fisher's proposed design used eight cups: four with milk poured first and four with tea first, presented in random order. She would be told there were four of each and asked to pick out the four milk-first cups. If she got them all right, would that be convincing? That depends on how easily a guesser could do the same, which turns out to be a question of counting."],
    ["One chance in 70", "There are 70 ways to choose four cups from eight, so a pure guesser gets them all right only once in 70.",
      "How many different ways can you choose four cups out of eight? The answer is 70. Only one of those choices is completely correct, so someone guessing at random has a 1 in 70 chance of a perfect score, about 1.4 per cent. That's unlikely enough that a perfect result would be reasonable evidence of real skill. But what about getting three right and one wrong? There are 16 ways to do that, so the chance of scoring at least three out of four by luck is 17 in 70, about 24 per cent. That's far too common to prove anything. Fisher's point was that you should decide what result would count as convincing before you run the test."],
    ["Why randomise?", "Random order stops hidden patterns or clues from masquerading as skill.",
      "Why insist the cups be served in random order? Imagine the four milk-first cups were all prepared first and served first. They might be slightly cooler, or the taster might pick up on a pattern. Any success could then come from those clues rather than from tasting the milk. Fisher argued that randomisation is the most reliable protection against hidden factors like these, including ones nobody has thought of. He also stressed keeping everything else the same: the same cups, the same tea and the same amount of milk. Fisher developed many of these ideas through crop experiments at Rothamsted, where treatments were assigned to plots of land at random so that differences in soil couldn't bias the results."],
    ["Size matters", "Too small a test can never give convincing evidence, however good the taster is.",
      "Fisher's framework also reveals why the number of trials matters. With six cups, three of each, there are only 20 ways to choose, so even a perfect score has a 1 in 20 chance of happening by luck, or five per cent. With just four cups, two of each, a guesser gets a perfect score one time in six. A test that small could never convince anyone, however genuine the skill. Fisher suggested treating results that would happen by chance less than about one time in 20 as worth taking seriously, a convention that later hardened into the famous 0.05 threshold for statistical significance. He saw it as a rough guide to which results deserve attention, not a magic line between truth and falsehood."],
    ["From tea to medicine", "Fisher's principles underpin modern clinical trials, field trials and online A/B tests.",
      "The ideas in Fisher's tea example now run through modern research. A drug trial assigns patients to treatment or placebo at random, works out in advance how many participants are needed, and asks how likely the observed results would be if the drug did nothing. Farmers test crop varieties in randomised plots. Technology companies run what they call A/B tests, randomly showing different versions of a web page to different users to see which works better. Even if you never calculate a probability, the core questions are worth carrying with you. What would I expect to see if this claim were false? Could something other than the claimed cause explain the result? And was the test big enough to tell?"],
    ["Even Fisher could be wrong", "A brilliant method doesn't protect its inventor from wishful thinking.",
      "Fisher was one of the most influential statisticians of the twentieth century and a major figure in genetics. Yet his story carries a warning too. In the late 1950s, as evidence mounted that smoking caused lung cancer, Fisher publicly argued that the link might not be causal. He suggested that some inherited factor could make people both more likely to smoke and more likely to develop cancer. Asking about a hidden common cause is a legitimate question, but the evidence that smoking itself was responsible soon became overwhelming. Fisher was a keen pipe smoker and had acted as a consultant to the tobacco industry. Good methods matter, but so does honesty about what you want to be true."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "What is the null hypothesis in the tea test?", ["That she can always tell the difference", "That she has no ability and is simply guessing", "That milk-first tea tastes better", "That the cups were poured incorrectly"], 1, "The null hypothesis assumes no real effect. The test then asks whether her results would be surprising if that were true."],
    [2, "With eight cups, four of each, what's the chance a pure guesser picks all four milk-first cups correctly?", ["1 in 8", "1 in 16", "1 in 70", "1 in 2"], 2, "There are 70 ways to choose four cups from eight, and only one of them is entirely correct."],
    [3, "Why should the cups be served in random order?", ["So hidden clues or patterns can't be mistaken for skill", "To make the test quicker", "To reduce the number of cups needed", "So the taster can't drink too much tea"], 0, "Randomisation protects against hidden factors, such as temperature or serving order, that could otherwise explain a good score."],
    [4, "Why would a test with only four cups, two of each, be unconvincing?", ["The tea would go cold before the end", "Fisher's maths only works with eight cups", "Small tests are always biased against the taster", "A guesser would get a perfect score one time in six"], 3, "With only six possible choices, even a perfect result could easily happen by luck, so it couldn't demonstrate real skill."],
    [6, "What did Fisher argue about smoking and lung cancer?", ["That smoking was clearly the main cause", "That an inherited factor might explain the link instead", "That lung cancer rates weren't really rising", "That no further research was needed"], 1, "Fisher proposed a hidden common cause. The question was fair, but the evidence soon showed smoking itself was responsible."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });
